        "stock/fujifilm-neopan-100-acros-ii":
            return "Fujifilm Neopan 100 Acros II"
        "stock/fujifilm-velvia-50":
            return "Fujifilm Velvia 50"
        "stock/kodak-exr-200t-5293":
            return "Kodak EXR 200T 5293"
        "stock/kodak-exr-500t-5298":
            return "Kodak EXR 500T 5298"
        "stock/kodak-vision-320t-5277":
            return "Kodak Vision 320T 5277"
        "stock/kodak-vision-500t-5279":
            return "Kodak Vision 500T 5279"
        _:
            return ""

## The essay text for `key`, or "".
pub func essay_text(key: str) -> str:
    match key:
        "process/bleach-bypass":
            return "In a normal color process, the bleach turns all the developed silver back into halide and the fixer washes it away, leaving only the dyes. Skip the bleach, or weaken it, and a black-and-white silver image stays on top of the color one. Kodak puts it simply: the retained silver increases contrast and decreases color saturation.\n\nThe cinematographer Kazuo Miyagawa, who had trained as a chemist, invented it for Kon Ichikawa's Her Brother in 1960. Labs later made it controllable: Technicolor's ENR, named for Ernesto Novelli Rimo, who devised it for Vittorio Storaro on Reds, and Deluxe's CCE, which left about three quarters of the silver in the prints of Seven. Saving Private Ryan used one of the strongest ENR levels Technicolor had run, to drain color from faces.\n\nDone to the negative, as here, the silver sits where the negative is dense: in the scene's highlights. The lab's scan, set for a bleached negative, reads it as extra exposure, so highlights crowd together and colors fade. Labs advise underexposing about a stop. The silver can be bleached later, so a bypassed negative is not lost."
        "process/c-41":
            return "C-41 is the color negative process of still photography, introduced by Kodak in 1972 together with Kodacolor II. It runs at 37.8 °C (100 °F) with a developer time of three minutes fifteen seconds, and every minilab in the world could run it, which made color negative film the film of family snapshots for decades.\n\nIts developing agent, CD-4, is more active than ECN-2's. Still films are made for it, and need no rem-jet removal. Motion picture film run through C-41, as some labs do, comes out denser and more contrasty than in ECN-2, and its rem-jet has to be dealt with.\n\nKodak's push rule for C-41 is thirty seconds more per stop: 3:45 for one stop, 4:15 for two. A push raises contrast, the blue layer's most, and grain, but adds little real shadow speed: the shadows of an underexposed negative stay thin."
        "process/d-76-1-1":
            return "D-76 diluted with an equal part of water and thrown away after one use. Diluted, the sulfite that dissolves grain edges is weaker and the developer runs out sooner in dense areas, so Kodak notes \"greater sharpness\" and a \"slight increase in graininess\" against the stock solution.\n\nDevelopment takes longer, about ten and a half minutes for Acros II at 20 °C, and is less sensitive to temperature: Kodak's tables for diluted developers change about 5.5 % per degree, against 8 to 9 % for stock solutions. Many photographers use it as their everyday developer for its sharpness and its fresh, consistent results."
        "process/d-76":
            return "Kodak published D-76 in 1927, originally for motion picture negative film. It is a metol–hydroquinone developer with a large amount of sodium sulfite, about 100 grams per litre, which preserves it and also dissolves a little of the silver halide as it develops, softening the grain.\n\nD-76 became the reference developer of black-and-white photography: film speeds and grain are judged against it, and almost every data sheet gives its times. It gives full film speed, moderate grain and good sharpness, a balance few developers improve on in every respect at once. Ilford's ID-11 is essentially the same formula.\n\nDiluted 1:1 and used once, it gives slightly sharper negatives. Time and temperature go together: the Acros II data sheet gives 8½ minutes at 18 °C and 4½ at 26 °C."
        "process/diafine":
            return "Diafine splits the developer in two. In the first bath the film soaks up developing agents, which cannot work without alkali; in the second, the alkali switches them on. The film develops only with what its emulsion absorbed, so where the image is dense the developer runs out, while the shadows develop fully.\n\nThat has two remarkable consequences. Time and temperature hardly matter: the maker says they have no practical effect between 21 and 29 °C. And films come out faster at a moderate, fixed contrast: the maker rates Tri-X at EI 1600, Acros at 200. Independent tests usually find about one stop rather than two for Tri-X, with highlights that never block up."
        "process/e-6":
            return "E-6 makes a positive slide in one film. The first developer is a black-and-white developer: it turns exposed grains into a silver negative image and uses them up. A reversal bath then fogs every grain that was not exposed (older processes used a flash of light). The color developer develops those grains, forming dyes where the scene was dark. Bleach and fix remove all the silver, and the dyes remain as a positive.\n\nEverything depends on the first developer, at 37.8 °C for six minutes. Developing it longer uses up more of the grains, so less dye forms and the slide gets lighter: that is how slides are pushed. Each stop of push costs some density in the blacks and shifts color slightly.\n\nE-6 replaced the older E-4 in the late 1970s and remains the process for every slide film still made, from Velvia to Ektachrome."
        "process/ecn-1":
            return "Before ECN-2, Eastman Color Negative was developed in ECN-1. Its color developer, SD-30, worked slowly and cool: about twelve minutes at 21 °C, where ECN-2 takes three minutes at 41 °C. Faster, hotter processes came in the 1970s as labs needed more throughput and Kodak's emulsions changed.\n\nThis was the process of the great 65 mm productions of the 1960s, among them 2001: A Space Odyssey, shot in Super Panavision 70 on Eastman Color Negative. Kodak's 1968 paper introducing the next negative, 5254, gives the ECN-1 sequence, and states that 5254's spectral sensitivities and dyes were the same as 5251's, the negative of that era."
        "process/ecn-2":
            return "ECN-2 is the process for motion picture color negative, used since the 1970s. The film first goes through a prebath that softens the rem-jet, the black anti-halation backing, which is then washed off. Then the color developer, CD-3, works for three minutes at 41.1 °C (106 °F): where it reduces exposed silver halide, its oxidized form couples with the dye couplers in each layer, building cyan, magenta and yellow dye around each developing grain.\n\nA stop bath ends development, a bleach turns the silver back into halide, and a fixer dissolves all the remaining halide away. What is left is the dye image and the orange mask, colored couplers that did not react and correct the dyes' unwanted absorptions.\n\nKodak's push times are 3:40 for one stop and 4:40 for two; a pull is 2:30. A push buys less than a third of a stop of real shadow speed for the first stop and about half a stop for two; the rest is contrast, grain, and a drift toward yellow highlights and blue shadows. The developer is held to a tenth of a degree, because a contrast mismatch between rolls would show from reel to reel."
        "process/hc-110-b":
            return "HC-110 is a syrupy concentrate, diluted just before use; dilution B (1+31) is the one Kodak's times are given for. Its developing agents sit in an organic solvent rather than water, so the concentrate keeps for years, which made it the developer of schools, newspapers and photographers who develop only now and then.\n\nDevelopment is quick, under four minutes for Tri-X at 20 °C, and gives full speed with a slightly coarser grain than D-76 and good sharpness. It is also more sensitive to temperature than most, close to ten percent per degree."
        "process/id-11":
            return "Ilford's ID-11 is essentially the same formula as Kodak's D-76: metol and hydroquinone with about 100 grams per litre of sodium sulfite. Ilford describes it as giving full emulsion speed with fine grain, and it has been the reference developer in Ilford's own data sheets for decades.\n\nIlford's rule for temperature is simple and generous: ten percent more time for each degree below 20 °C, ten percent less for each degree above. Acros II's data sheet gives it 6¾ minutes at 20 °C."
        "process/microfine":
            return "Fujifilm's own fine-grain developer, and the one its Acros data sheet measures granularity in: an RMS granularity of 7 for Acros II, among the finest figures of any black-and-white film. Unlike most fine-grain developers it costs Acros no speed: ten minutes at 20 °C at EI 100.\n\nFujifilm's table for it runs from twelve and a half minutes at 18 °C to under six at 26 °C, about ten percent per degree."
        "process/microphen":
            return "Microphen is Ilford's speed-increasing developer: phenidone and hydroquinone, buffered with borax at low alkalinity. Phenidone, discovered at Ilford by J. D. Kendall in 1940, is barely held back by the bromide that development releases, so development keeps going in the deepest shadows.\n\nIlford claims up to half a stop more speed than ID-11 with comparable grain, a high \"speed to grain ratio\". It is the developer Ilford recommends for pushing HP5 Plus, with times given up to EI 3200."
        "process/perceptol":
            return "Perceptol is a fine-grain developer of the solvent type, like Kodak's Microdol-X: metol with a large amount of sulfite and some chloride, at a nearly neutral pH. It dissolves a good part of each grain as it develops, and that silver is redeposited as fine particles, so the image is made of smaller specks.\n\nThe price is speed. Ilford rates HP5 Plus at EI 250 and Tri-X at EI 200 in Perceptol, against 400 in ID-11. Photographers use it when the finest grain matters more than speed: large prints from 35 mm, smooth skies, portraits."
        "process/pmk-pyro":
            return "Pyrogallol was one of the first developing agents, used from the 1850s. As it develops it oxidizes into products that stain and harden the gelatin around each grain, in proportion to the silver. Gordon Hutchings revived it in the 1980s as PMK (Pyro, Metol, Kodalk), described in The Book of Pyro, and it became a favourite of large-format photographers.\n\nThe stain does two things. It masks the grain, so prints look smoother than the silver alone would. And it is yellow-green, so it adds printing density in the highlights mainly for blue-sensitive papers, compressing them. The hardened gelatin also limits the sideways diffusion of developer, which gives sharp, well-separated edges."
        "process/rodinal-1-100-stand":
            return "A very dilute developer left almost untouched: the film is agitated at the start and then stands for an hour. Where the image is dense, the developer around it is exhausted within minutes and development stops; in the shadows it continues. The result is strong compensation, highlights held back and shadows built up, and strong edge effects as exhausted developer and released bromide spread around dense areas.\n\nAgfa's own figures for 1+100 show real speed loss, Tri-X at about EI 250. Stand development has risks: bromide-laden developer can stream down from dense areas, leaving streaks known as bromide drag. Photographers use it for scenes of extreme contrast and for its etched, glowing edges."
        "process/rodinal-1-25":
            return "Rodinal is one of the oldest photographic products still made: Momme Andresen patented it for Agfa in 1891. It is a concentrate of p-aminophenol in strong alkali with almost no sulfite, diluted just before use.\n\nWith little sulfite there is no solvent action on the grain. Each grain develops whole and sharp-edged, so the grain is crisp and clearly defined, and Agfa sold it for its \"very good contour sharpness\". It costs a little speed, about a third of a stop. Its look is the classic one of gritty, sharp black-and-white: every grain visible, every edge clear."
        "process/rodinal-1-50":
            return "Rodinal diluted twice as far. The weaker developer exhausts quickly in dense areas: highlights are held back while shadows keep developing, which compresses a contrasty scene, and fresh developer diffusing into a thin area beside a dense one makes edges stand out, the Eberhard effect.\n\nDevelopment takes about twelve minutes for Tri-X at 20 °C, rated around EI 400 at best. It is a favourite for its sharpness and tonality, with grain as defined as at 1+25."
        "process/t-max-developer":
            return "A liquid developer Kodak made for its T-MAX films, whose flat, tabular \"T-grain\" crystals capture light efficiently but need a developer that brings out their full speed. Kodak describes it as giving enhanced shadow detail, at normal ratings and when pushing.\n\nIt is used at 1+4 and gives roughly a third of a stop more shadow speed than D-76, with a little more grain. T-MAX P3200, a film meant to be pushed, was usually developed in it."
        "process/xtol-1-1":
            return "XTOL diluted with an equal part of water and used once. Kodak's sheet promises \"slightly greater film speed\" and \"enhanced sharpness\" than the stock solution, with slightly more grain: the usual trade of dilution, where a weaker developer exhausts locally at edges and brings out fine detail.\n\nLike other dilutions it is less sensitive to temperature, about 5.5 % per degree, which makes it forgiving in a home darkroom."
        "process/xtol":
            return "XTOL, introduced by Kodak in 1996, replaced the hydroquinone of classic developers with ascorbate, vitamin C, and uses a phenidone-type agent, Dimezone S. It is low in toxicity and was designed to give more from the same film.\n\nKodak claimed that most films gain about a third of a stop of real speed in XTOL, and that prints can be ten percent larger for the same grain and sharpness. It became a favourite for modern T-grain films and for scanning. Its one well-known weakness was sudden failure: an exhausted or contaminated batch could stop developing without warning, so careful users tested each batch."
        "stage/develop":
            return "The developer is a reducing agent. It turns exposed grains, those carrying a latent image, into metallic silver, and leaves unexposed grains alone, at least for a while. Given enough time, it starts on them too, which shows as fog.\n\nHow far development goes depends on time, temperature and agitation. Longer, warmer or more agitated development produces more density, mostly in the highlights, so contrast rises. It also lets weakly exposed grains develop, which effectively raises the film's speed. That is a push: rate the film faster, underexpose, and develop longer. A pull does the opposite.\n\nIn color development, the developer that has given up its electrons reacts with the couplers next to the grain and forms dye. The silver is later bleached away, leaving only the dye clouds, which is why color grain looks soft and round.\n\nHere, time and temperature start at the process's own normal, from the manufacturer's tables."
        "stage/easel":
            return "In the darkroom, the easel holds the paper under the enlarger and its blades frame the picture. Framing is the last decision, and the photographer's alone.\n\nHere, the easel crops the frame, corrects perspective and rotation (the guides find the lines that should be vertical or horizontal), and darkens or lightens the edges, as printers burned in the corners of a print with their hands or a card to hold the eye in the frame.\n\nBecause it comes last, the easel works on the finished picture. Cropping here does not change how the film was exposed or how big its grain is: the grain belongs to the negative and its format, chosen in the Film node."
        "stage/exposure":
            return "Exposure is light times time: how bright the image on the film plane is, and for how long the shutter lets it fall. Photographers count it in stops. One stop is a doubling or a halving.\n\nNegative film is forgiving. A color negative records about ten stops or more, and two stops of overexposure often look better than a normal exposure, with denser, cleaner shadows. Underexposure is what hurts: shadow detail never reaches the film, and the grain of a thin negative shows.\n\nSlide film is the opposite. Its range is about five or six stops, and highlights burn out with little warning. Slide shooters often underexposed by a third of a stop to keep color deep.\n\nHere, Exposure changes the light reaching the film, and nothing later takes it back. Underexpose and then push in the Develop node, and you see what a pushed negative really looks like."
        "stage/file":
            return "A raw file holds what each photosite of the sensor counted: photons, nearly in proportion to the light that fell on it. Nothing has been decided yet. There is no white balance, no contrast curve and no color rendering, only a record of the scene's light behind a mosaic of red, green and blue filters.\n\nThat is why the recipe starts here. Developing the raw recovers the scene's light as numbers that still add up the way light does: twice the light is twice the value. Everything after this node treats those numbers as light falling on a lens and then on film, just as the real scene once did.\n\nA JPEG is different. It has already been through a camera's own rendering, so it is turned back into light first (the inverse of a display transform), which works well for textures and screen content but cannot bring back what the camera threw away."
        "stage/film":
            return "Film is a thin plastic base coated with gelatin holding crystals of silver halide, the grains. A photon absorbed by a grain can leave a few atoms of metallic silver, a latent image too small to see. Bigger grains catch light more easily, so fast films have bigger grains and show more grain.\n\nColor film stacks three such layers, made sensitive by dyes to blue, green and red light, each holding a coupler that will become a dye: yellow, magenta and cyan. Between and within the layers, chemistry links them. Development in one layer releases inhibitors that hold back its neighbors, the interimage effect, which makes film colors richer than a simple mix.\n\nLight also scatters inside the emulsion and some bounces back off the base: halation, the warm glow around bright lights. Here each stock is built from its manufacturer's published data: the layers' spectral sensitivities, characteristic curves, dyes, sharpness and granularity."
        "stage/filter":
            return "A filter changes the light before the film sees it. Two families matter here.\n\nConversion filters fix a mismatch between the light and the film. The orange 85 turns daylight into something close to the 3200 K that tungsten stock expects, and costs two thirds of a stop. Without it, tungsten film outdoors goes blue.\n\nBlack-and-white photographers use strong color filters to change which colors become light or dark grey. A yellow 8 darkens a blue sky a little, as the eye expects. An orange 21 darkens it more and lightens skin. A red 25 turns the sky almost black and makes clouds dramatic; Ansel Adams made Monolith, the Face of Half Dome, with a deep red filter. A green 11 lightens foliage. A blue 47 does the opposite of the red.\n\nEvery filter takes away light. The filter factor is how much more exposure it needs, and this node opens up by that factor, as the photographer would."
        "stage/grade":
            return "Once the negative is scanned, it is images again, and a colorist shapes them. On film-originated television this happened on the telecine itself, with controllers such as the da Vinci, adjusting lift, gamma and gain per color and per scene.\n\nThe tools here are the familiar ones: tone (contrast, highlights, shadows, whites and blacks), levels, curves, vibrance and saturation, a mixer of eight hue bands, color wheels for shadows, midtones and highlights, and a black-and-white conversion with its own channel weights.\n\nA grade cannot add what the film did not record. It can, however, push a look much further than any lab could, which is why this stage sits after the scan: the film decides the material, the grade decides the picture."
        "stage/lens":
            return "Every lens lets less light reach the corners of the frame than the center. Part of it is geometry: light reaching the edge arrives at an angle, spread over more area and seen through a narrower opening, which by itself dims it by about the fourth power of the cosine of the angle. Part of it is the lens barrel cutting off oblique light, worst at wide apertures.\n\nCinematographers lived with it and often liked it: the gentle darkening draws the eye to the middle of the frame. Modern raw developers usually correct it away. Here it happens to the light before the film sees it, so the darker corners also get the film's response there, a slightly thinner negative and a touch more grain, rather than a vignette painted on afterwards."
        "stage/lightbox":
            return "A slide is the picture itself. Reversal film comes out of processing as a positive, so there is nothing to print or invert: it is viewed by light shining through it, on a light table with a loupe, or projected.\n\nLight tables for judging color are made to a standard, D50 light, close to daylight at 5000 K, so that everyone in a print shop or an agency saw the same slide the same way. Seen like this, a slide shows its full range: the deepest dye densities of a good slide reach beyond 3.5, far more than a print on paper can hold.\n\nHere, the slide is lit by D50 light and the light coming through its dyes is what is shown."
        "stage/print":
            return "In the cinema chain, the camera negative was printed onto print film: a fine-grained, high-contrast stock exposed through the negative by a printer, then developed. The print is a positive, meant to be projected.\n\nThe lab controlled color with printer lights: the red, green and blue intensities of the printer lamp, in steps called points. A timer chose them shot by shot, which is where the term color timing comes from. Raising a light adds density in that color's dye on the print.\n\nPrint film is steep: its contrast multiplies the negative's, and its dyes give the deep blacks and saturated, slightly crushed color of a film print. Kodak Vision Color Print Film 2383 was the release print stock of most films from the late 1990s on, and it is the print stock here."
        "stage/project":
            return "A film print is made to be seen by projection. A xenon arc lamp, close to daylight in color, shines through the print and a lens throws the image onto a screen many meters away.\n\nThe dark theater matters. With no other light, the eye adapts to the screen, and the print's dense blacks look truly black. Some light always strays, from the lens, the screen and the room, and lifts the deepest shadows a little: projection flare.\n\nProjected film has a look of its own: rich, saturated midtones, highlights that roll off rather than clip, and shadows that sink. This node shows the print as it would look on such a screen."
        "stage/scan":
            return "A negative is not a picture yet. It is inverted, and color negatives carry an orange mask, extra dye that corrects for the cyan and magenta dyes absorbing light they should not. To see the image, the film is read as densities and turned back into light.\n\nFor television, that was the telecine: a Rank Cintel flying-spot scanner, and later the Spirit DataCine, reading the negative straight to video. A colorist set the transfer per scene: how warm, how dense, where black sits. Modern film scanners read printing density, the densities print film would see, so a scan can stand in for a print.\n\nThe scan here measures the negative through the densities its dyes produce and maps them back to scene light, set up once for a normally exposed and developed negative. Timing (Warmth and Tint), Contrast and Black adjust it as the telecine operator would."
        "stage/view":
            return "A display cannot show the light a scan holds. Scene light ranges over many stops; a screen has a fixed white and a limited range. A view transform decides how one maps to the other, rolling off highlights, keeping midtones and choosing how saturated colors are shown.\n\nACES 2.0 is the Academy Color Encoding System's current output transform, used across the film industry, with highlights that desaturate gracefully as they approach white. Standard is a plainer filmic curve. Linear shows the values as they are, clipped.\n\nIn the cinema chain this node is replaced by Print and Project, which are their own view transform: the print film's curve and the theater."
        "stage/white_balance":
            return "Light has a color. A tungsten lamp burns at about 3200 K and gives warm, orange light; daylight is near 5500 to 6500 K and bluer. Our eyes adapt and see a white shirt as white under both. Film does not adapt: it is made for one kind of light.\n\nMotion picture stocks come in two balances. Tungsten (T) stocks such as Vision3 500T expect 3200 K studio lamps; daylight (D) stocks expect the sun. Shooting tungsten film outdoors gives a strong blue cast unless an orange 85 filter is put on the lens, at the cost of two thirds of a stop.\n\nHere, Temperature and Tint describe the light the scene was lit by. Left as shot, they are the camera's own estimate. With the Filter node's Balance set to Matched, the light is balanced to the stock, as a lab would; set to None, the film sees the light as it was, color cast and all."
        "stock/fujifilm-neopan-100-acros-ii":
            return "Acros was Fujifilm's fine-grained ISO 100 black-and-white film, known for some of the finest grain and best sharpness of any conventional black-and-white film, and for remarkable reciprocity: it needs no exposure correction for exposures up to two minutes, which made it a favourite for night photography and long exposures.\n\nFujifilm stopped making it in 2018, and Acros II appeared in 2019, made with a new emulsion technology Fujifilm calls P.I.D.C. Its data sheet gives characteristic curves for D-76 at 4, 7 and 10 minutes, which this simulator uses to model how development time changes contrast and speed, and a granularity of 7 in Microfine developer.\n\nAcros' grain is a fine, tight texture, which is why Fujifilm's digital cameras imitate it with a grain of their own. Here its grain comes from small, crisp clouds of silver on a single layer."
        "stock/fujifilm-velvia-50":
            return "When Fujifilm introduced Velvia in 1990, it changed landscape and nature photography. Its colors are far more saturated than the slide films before it, especially greens and reds, with deep blacks and very fine grain. It was rated ISO 50, and many photographers rated it at 40.\n\nVelvia is a reversal film: it is developed in E-6 into a slide, a positive to be seen on a light table or projected. It has a narrow exposure range, about five stops, and little forgiveness for overexposure, so landscape photographers metered carefully and shot in soft light, often with graduated filters to hold the sky.\n\nThe original was discontinued in 2005 because some of its materials became unavailable, and Velvia 50 returned in 2007 in a new formulation made to look the same. The curves here come from that film's data sheet."
        "stock/kodak-exr-200t-5293":
            return "The medium-speed tungsten stock of the EXR line, rated EI 200 at 3200 K. With less speed it has finer grain than 500T, and it was a workhorse of studio productions in the early and mid 1990s, both for features and for television.\n\nDirectors of photography on television valued its slightly higher contrast: material that is going to be transferred, compressed and broadcast tends to lose snap along the way, so a negative that starts a little punchier survives better. The DP of Spin City chose it for exactly that reason.\n\nThe data sheet for 5293 also publishes the spectral densities of its cyan, magenta and yellow dyes. They are the dyes of the ECN-2 process, which this simulator uses for its EXR stocks."
        "stock/kodak-exr-500t-5298":
            return "EXR was Kodak's motion picture negative line of the late 1980s and early 1990s. Its 500T was the fast, tungsten-balanced member, rated EI 500 under 3200 K studio light and EI 320 in daylight behind an 85 filter. Kodak's 1993 data sheet for 5298 is the source of the curves used here.\n\nIt is a stock of its time. Its red layer is noticeably softer than its green and blue, its blue layer the grainiest, and it carries a rem-jet backing, a black layer on the back that stops light reflecting off the base, so it shows little halation.\n\nFast tungsten negative like this was the natural choice for 1990s American television shot on 35 mm, such as Friends: sets lit by tungsten lamps, a negative telecined straight to video, never printed. No source names the stock Friends used in each season; 5298 and its slower sibling 5293 are the likely candidates for the early years."
        "stock/kodak-vision-320t-5277":
            return "Vision 320T was made for television. Kodak designed it with a lower contrast than its feature stocks, because a telecine transfer and a television screen both add contrast of their own; starting flatter keeps detail in the faces and in the shadows of a set.\n\nIts 320 speed sat between the 200 and 500 stocks, fast enough for a sitcom's lighting with good depth of field across a stage. Shows such as Everybody Loves Raymond used it. Its look is clean, slightly soft in contrast, and it takes a grade well."
        "stock/kodak-vision-500t-5279":
            return "In 1996 Kodak began replacing EXR with Vision, and 5279 was the first: a 500-speed tungsten negative with finer grain than the EXR 500T it succeeded. Its sheet places a normal grey exposure about four stops above the film's threshold, which shows how much shadow latitude a negative carries below a correctly exposed face.\n\nVision 500T became one of the most used stocks of the late 1990s and early 2000s, in features, commercials and television. It was followed by Vision2 500T 5218 in 2002 and Vision3 500T 5219 in 2007, still in production today. Each generation kept the look recognizably Kodak, warm and smooth, with less grain and more highlight latitude."
        _:
            return ""
